          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What HbA1c target does guidance describe, and is there one number everybody uses?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The guidance I could retrieve does not give one fixed number for everyone. It describes a general goal and says it should be individualized.\n\nThe main source is the national diabetes association's standards of care summary, \"Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals.\" It describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. It also says a stricter goal, such as below 6.5 percent, may be appropriate where it can be reached without significant low blood sugar or other harm. That is more likely for someone with a shorter history of diabetes and a longer life expectancy. The retrieved text was cut off, so I could not read the full list of who might need a looser goal.\n\nThat same document is flagged as having a section on where guidance differs, but that section was not in the text I received. So I cannot tell you how other authorities compare on this number.\n\nA separate guideline on HbA1c testing, \"Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds,\" adds one caution. HbA1c can be unreliable with hemoglobin variants, recent blood transfusion, iron-deficiency anemia, pregnancy, blood-cell breakdown, or advanced kidney disease. In those cases, glucose-based tests are used instead.\n\nThe right target for a specific person is a decision to make with their clinician, who can weigh their age, health, and risk of low blood sugar.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
